---
import EnglishBreadcrumb from '../../../../components/english/EnglishBreadcrumb.astro'
import { IncrementalVsFullLoadLab } from '../../../../components/english/IncrementalVsFullLoadLab'
import { tradeoffData } from '../../../../features/performance/banking'
import { buildIncrementalLoadComparison } from '../../../../features/english-load-comparison/model'
import EnglishArticleLayout from '../../../../layouts/EnglishArticleLayout.astro'
import incrementalLoadStylesUrl from '../../../../styles/layouts/english-incremental-load.css?url'
import { getEnglishRoute } from '../../../../utils/english-routes'

const title = 'Incremental vs Full Load: Scope & Late Data | sql.sb'
const description =
  'Compare incremental and full loads by recompute scope, late-arriving data and equivalence checks—not by write policy alone.'

const comparison = buildIncrementalLoadComparison()
const fullLoad = comparison.plans.find(({ id }) => id === 'full-load')!
const eventTimeOnly = comparison.plans.find(({ id }) => id === 'event-time-only')!
const arrivalCursor = comparison.plans.find(({ id }) => id === 'arrival-cursor')!
const lateData = tradeoffData.lateData
const usd = (value: number) => value.toLocaleString('en-US', { maximumFractionDigits: 0 })
---

<EnglishArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  active="warehouse"
  styleUrls={[incrementalLoadStylesUrl]}
>
  <EnglishBreadcrumb
    items={[
      { label: 'English home', key: 'overview' },
      { label: 'Data warehouse', key: 'warehouse' },
      { label: 'Incremental vs full load' },
    ]}
  />

  <header class="english-article__header">
    <p class="english-article__eyebrow">Data warehouse · pipeline design</p>
    <h1 class="english-article__title">
      Incremental load vs full load: how much should you recompute?
    </h1>
    <p class="english-article__lede">
      A smaller load is useful only when it still includes every changed business result. Compare
      the work each plan repeats, then check the incremental answer against a full-load reference.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="english-answer">
    <span class="english-answer__label">Quick answer</span>
    <p>
      A <strong>full load</strong> rebuilds the complete declared target scope from the available
      source state. An <strong>incremental load</strong> processes a smaller set of new or changed
      data and recomputes only the output scope that data can affect. Incremental processing can
      reduce repeated work, but only if its change cursor, late-data policy and dependency range are
      complete. A late record for an old business date may require rebuilding that date—or a wider
      downstream range. Load scope is separate from whether repeating a target write is safe; see{' '}
      <a href={getEnglishRoute('idempotentEtl')}>idempotent ETL</a> for write semantics.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="english-article__body">
    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="scope">
      <h2 id="scope">Load scope is a recomputation decision</h2>
      <p>
        “Incremental” does not mean “append only,” and “full” does not mean “delete everything.”
        Here, the terms describe how much source state is read and how much target output is
        recalculated. The target write method is a separate decision.
      </p>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>What each load plan recalculates</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Plan</th>
              <th scope="col">Input scope</th>
              <th scope="col">Output scope</th>
              <th scope="col">Main condition</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Full load</th>
              <td>Complete source scope at a declared cutoff</td>
              <td>Every output partition in that scope</td>
              <td>Rebuild cost and runtime fit the operating window</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Incremental load</th>
              <td>New or changed records after a durable source cursor</td>
              <td>Every partition or downstream range those changes affect</td>
              <td>Capture, correction and reconciliation rules are explicit</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        A source cursor answers <em>what arrived or changed since the prior run</em>. A
        business-date partition answers <em>which historical output must be recalculated</em>. Those
        are different columns and may move in opposite directions when data arrives late.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="example">
      <h2 id="example">A late change can belong to an older event-time window</h2>
      <p>
        The small change-log fixture below begins with three records available at{' '}
        {comparison.initialAsOf}. Two more changes become available by {comparison.finalAsOf}: one
        inserts a transaction with an earlier event time, and one updates an earlier transaction.
        The initial event-time watermark is {comparison.eventTimeWatermark}; neither change is
        beyond it.
      </p>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Window totals before and after the late changes</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Event-time window</th>
              <th scope="col">Initial result</th>
              <th scope="col">Full-load reference</th>
              <th scope="col">Event-time watermark only</th>
              <th scope="col">Arrival cursor + affected window</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {comparison.windows.map((window, index) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{window.label}</th>
                <td>{usd(comparison.initialOutput[index]?.amountTotal ?? 0)}</td>
                <td>{usd(fullLoad.output[index]?.amountTotal ?? 0)}</td>
                <td>{usd(eventTimeOnly.output[index]?.amountTotal ?? 0)}</td>
                <td>{usd(arrivalCursor.output[index]?.amountTotal ?? 0)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
          <tfoot>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Total amount</th>
              <td>
                {usd(comparison.initialOutput.reduce((total, row) => total + row.amountTotal, 0))}
              </td>
              <td>{usd(fullLoad.totalAmount)}</td>
              <td>{usd(eventTimeOnly.totalAmount)}</td>
              <td>{usd(arrivalCursor.totalAmount)}</td>
            </tr>
          </tfoot>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The event-time-only filter leaves the old result at {usd(eventTimeOnly.totalAmount)}.
        Reading changes after the arrival cursor finds both late changes, identifies the affected
        10:00–10:05 window, and rebuilds that window from its input. It reads{' '}
        {arrivalCursor.sourceRowsRecomputed}
        fixture records and recomputes one of two windows; the full-load reference reads{' '}
        {fullLoad.sourceRowsRecomputed} fixture records and recomputes both. Both correct outputs
        are {usd(fullLoad.totalAmount)}. These small counts are for checking logic, not physical
        I/O, runtime, or a production benchmark.
      </p>
      <IncrementalVsFullLoadLab client:load />
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="business-date">
      <h2 id="business-date">Arrival date is not business date</h2>
      <p>
        The existing banking performance fixture gives a calendar-date example: a transaction is
        received on {lateData.receivedAt} but belongs to business date {lateData.businessDate} for
        customer {lateData.customerId} and counterparty {lateData.counterpartyId}.
      </p>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>One late row, two different dates</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Received at</th>
              <th scope="col">Business date</th>
              <th scope="col">Example effect</th>
              <th scope="col">Affected output range</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>{lateData.receivedAt}</td>
              <td>{lateData.businessDate}</td>
              <td>
                {lateData.customerId} × {lateData.counterpartyId}: first-seen date changes from{' '}
                {lateData.currentFirstSeenDate} to {lateData.correctedFirstSeenDate}
              </td>
              <td>{lateData.affectedFeatureRange}</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        If the target is a daily total, the repair may be limited to the affected business-date
        partition. A cumulative feature can have a wider dependency range: the first-seen correction
        above can change later customer-day results too. Derive the recompute scope from the
        target's meaning and dependencies; do not assume that “last day” or a fixed lookback is
        sufficient. For downstream replay order and verification, see{' '}
        <a href={getEnglishRoute('pipelineFailureRecovery')}>pipeline failure recovery</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="implementation">
      <h2 id="implementation">A practical incremental pattern</h2>
      <p>
        First capture changes using an arrival or source-version cursor. Next derive the affected
        business dates from those changes. Then read the complete source rows for each affected date
        and recompute the target grain. The cursor should advance only after the run's data and
        evidence are safely committed; the mechanics depend on the source and warehouse.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`WITH changed_rows AS (
  SELECT *
  FROM source_change_log
  WHERE (available_at, change_id) > (:saved_available_at, :saved_change_id)
),
affected_dates AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT business_date
  FROM changed_rows
),
recomputed_daily AS (
  SELECT source.business_date, SUM(source.amount) AS daily_amount
  FROM source
  JOIN affected_dates USING (business_date)
  GROUP BY source.business_date
)
SELECT * FROM recomputed_daily;`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        This is a scope sketch, not portable SQL: tuple comparisons, CDC metadata, tombstones and
        transaction boundaries vary by engine. For updates or deletes, the change feed must reveal
        enough information to identify every affected key and date. A corrected cursor without a
        complete change feed can still miss data.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="tradeoffs">
      <h2 id="tradeoffs">When is a full load the better choice?</h2>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Operational trade-offs, not a universal winner</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Consideration</th>
              <th scope="col">Full load</th>
              <th scope="col">Incremental load</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Compute and scan</th>
              <td>Repeats work across the declared source and target scope</td>
              <td>Can reduce repeated work when changed scope is small</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Late data</th>
              <td>Included if the full reference reads the corrected source state</td>
              <td>Needs a change cursor plus affected-date or dependency-range repair</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Complexity</th>
              <td>Often fewer state boundaries; may be too expensive at scale</td>
              <td>Requires cursor durability, change capture, backfill and equivalence checks</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Good fit</th>
              <td>Small source, manageable runtime, or a trusted rebuild/reference path</td>
              <td>Large repeated scans, bounded changes, and owned repair procedures</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Incremental is not automatically cheaper end to end: change capture, state, storage, repair
        runs and validation also consume resources. A full load is often a valuable reconciliation
        reference even when it is not the routine schedule. Confirm the actual scan, compute, SLA
        and freshness behavior in the target engine; this fixture does not establish a performance
        win.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="boundary">
      <h2 id="boundary">Do not confuse load scope with idempotent writes</h2>
      <p>
        Incremental versus full asks <em>which source and output range to recompute</em>.
        Idempotence asks <em>what happens if the same result is written again</em>. A full load can
        still append duplicate rows, and an incremental load can still be safely repeatable—or not.
        Choose and test both contracts independently in{' '}
        <a href={getEnglishRoute('idempotentEtl')}>how to make an ETL job idempotent</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fixture's cursor-and-window result matches the full-load reference; the event-time-only
        result does not. A production equivalence check should compare the declared grain, keys,
        nulls, deletes and relevant measures over a stated cutoff—not just the row count.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="english-article__related" aria-label="Related English data warehouse pages">
      <a href={getEnglishRoute('warehouse')}>All data warehouse pages</a>
      <a href={getEnglishRoute('idempotentEtl')}>Idempotent ETL and write safety</a>
      <a href={getEnglishRoute('pipelineFailureRecovery')}>Pipeline failure recovery</a>
      <a href={getEnglishRoute('pipelineObservability')}>Pipeline observability</a>
      <a href={getEnglishRoute('grain')}>Data warehouse grain</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</EnglishArticleLayout>
